In this lesson, we'll look at how to find the distance between any two points on a coordinate plane. It all comes straight from Pythagoras' theorem. Imagine two points on a grid. You can draw a right-angled triangle between them, where one leg is the horizontal gap between the points, and the other leg is the vertical gap. The distance between the points is just the length of the triangle's hypotenuse, so the distance squared equals the horizontal gap squared plus the vertical gap squared. One nice thing about this formula is that the squares inside the square root are always positive, so the distance you calculate will always be a positive number, never negative. There are also a couple of handy special cases to remember. If the two points sit on the same horizontal line, their heights match, so the distance is just the absolute horizontal gap between them. And if they sit on the same vertical line, the distance is just the absolute vertical gap. Now let's see it in action.